TA-kernel
====

Collects kernel crash-readiness and crash signals: kexec crash kernel loaded, kdump service state, kernel taint state, uptime, and counts of NEW oops/panic/BUG lines in the kernel journal ring since the previous cycle. Emits JSON events to index=main (sourcetype kernel:status) and kernel.* metrics to index=metrics (sourcetype kernel:metrics); alarm status when new oops/panic/BUG lines are detected.

Prerequisites
-------------
  * systemd-journald (kernel log in the journal)
  * kdump service is checked but not required to be enabled
  * Splunk Enterprise or Universal Forwarder 9.x - 10.6 (forwarder recommended
    on monitored hosts).
  * A metrics-typed index (default name: metrics) on the indexer:

      [metrics]
      datatype = metric

    Forwarders only need that index to exist on the indexer side.
  * No network egress, no root requirement beyond the Splunk service account.
    Fully standalone add-on with no dependencies.

Install
-------
Install on each monitored host (Apps > Install app from file, splunk install
app, or deployment server), restart Splunk, then verify:

  index=main sourcetype="kernel:status" | head

Search quick reference
----------------------
  index=main sourcetype="kernel:status" | head
  | mcatalog values(metric_name) where index=metrics metric_name="kernel.*" by host
  | mstats avg("kernel.*") where index=metrics span=60s by host

Usage
-----
Events: index=main sourcetype=kernel:status  (kexec_crash_loaded, kdump_active, taint, uptime_s, new counts)
Metrics: kernel.kexec_crash_loaded, kernel.kdump_active, kernel.tainted, kernel.oops_new, kernel.panic_new
Alarm status when new oops/panic/BUG lines appear since the previous run.
Dashboards: TA-Kernel > Kernel Overview / Kernel Metrics

Configure
---------
Override the interval or destination index in etc/apps/TA-kernel/local/inputs.conf
(never edit default/).

Troubleshooting
---------------
  * State file under /var/tmp holds the last-seen journal position; first run after install establishes a baseline.
  * Kernel logs must be in the journal (default on systemd hosts).

